<TopicPage slug="subjects/biology/foundational/biological-molecules">
  <h2>The idea: four families of molecule</h2>
  <p>
    Cells build everything from four families of biological molecule, and you can tell the
    families apart by their elements (unit 4.1, Core): <strong>carbohydrates</strong> and
    <strong>fats</strong> contain carbon, hydrogen and oxygen; <strong>proteins</strong> add
    nitrogen — and often sulfur — to that list. Each family is assembled from small units
    (Core point 2): <strong>starch, glycogen and cellulose</strong> are all chains of
    <strong>glucose</strong> — the same brick, three different buildings; <strong>proteins</strong>
    are chains of <strong>amino acids</strong> (twenty-odd kinds, strung in any order);
    <strong>fats and oils</strong> are built from <strong>fatty acids and glycerol</strong>.
    Big molecules from small ones, joined and split again by enzymes — that is the whole
    economy of the cell.
  </p>

  <p>
    The three glucose polymers repay a closer look, because identical bricks can build three
    utterly different buildings. <strong>Starch</strong> is the compact, slightly branched
    store plants fill their potatoes and seeds with; <strong>glycogen</strong> is the animal
    version — more heavily branched, so glucose can be snapped off quickly from many ends in
    your liver and muscles; <strong>cellulose</strong> uses a different linkage between the
    same glucose units, producing straight chains that hydrogen-bond into cables — the fibre
    in cell walls that we cannot digest but trees stand on. Proteins earn their versatility
    the same way: twenty kinds of amino-acid brick, folded into shapes that grip, gate,
    catalyse or brace. And fats store about twice the energy per gram as carbohydrate
    (roughly 39 kJ/g against 17 kJ/g), which is why seeds pack oil and your body banks
    fat rather than starch for lean times.
  </p>

  <h2>Water: the solvent everything happens in</h2>
  <p>
    None of this chemistry runs on dry molecules. Water is the medium of digestion, excretion
    and transport (unit 3.2 Core 1), and it earns the job by dissolving extraordinary amounts
    of solutes. Sucrose — the sugar plants ship around in phloem sap and we stir into tea —
    is one of the most soluble common solids known. Handbook data:
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="solubility of sucrose in water against temperature (CRC Handbook data) — hot water dissolves nearly three times more sugar than cold" widget="plotter" config={solubilityCfg} />

  <p>
    Trace the roles through one meal. <strong>Digestion</strong>: starch, proteins and fats
    are insoluble polymers, so enzymes must first break them into soluble glucose, amino acids
    and fatty acids — digestion is the chemistry of making food water-soluble.
    <strong>Transport</strong>: blood plasma carries glucose, amino acids, ions and urea in
    solution, and phloem sap ships sucrose the same way. <strong>Excretion</strong>: urea
    leaves the body dissolved in water — no solvent, no exit. Water's other talent, crossing
    partially permeable membranes by osmosis, is the subject of its own lesson.
  </p>

  <h2>How to identify them: the chemical tests</h2>
  <p>
    You cannot see molecules, but you can test for them. These are the five tests the syllabus
    requires (Core point 3) — each with its positive result, and each a classic practical:
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="five tests, five colour changes — the working detection kit of food science" widget="equationStep" config={testsCfg} />

  <p>
    A note on honesty in practicals: Benedict's test needs <em>heating</em> (a water bath at
    about 80 °C, not a naked flame on the tube), the biuret reagent is added drop by drop to
    avoid a false precipitate, and the ethanol emulsion test works only if the sample actually
    dissolves in the ethanol first. Negative results are still results — distilled water should
    fail all five, which is exactly why you run it as a control.
  </p>

  <h2>DNA: the molecule that instructs the others</h2>
  <p>
    One more molecule belongs to this lesson, because the syllabus puts it here (Supplement
    point 4). <strong>DNA</strong> is built from <strong>nucleotide</strong> units, but its
    shape is what matters: <strong>two strands coiled into a double helix</strong>, each strand
    carrying a sequence of <strong>bases</strong>, with <strong>bonds between paired bases</strong>
    holding the strands together. Pairing is strict: <strong>A pairs with T, and C pairs with
    G</strong>. That one rule makes the molecule endlessly copyable and endlessly readable —
    the order of bases spells the order of amino acids in every protein above.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Cambridge IGCSE Biology 0610 syllabus (2026–2028), unit 4; sucrose solubility data from CRC Handbook of Chemistry and Physics; OpenStax Biology 2e, ch. 3 (Biological Macromolecules); Campbell Biology (Urry et al.), 12th ed., ch. 4–5.</p>
